{"id":"sig-sg-550","slug":"sig-sg-550","name":"SIG SG 550","aliases":["SG551","SG552","SG 550","SG 552","SIG552","SG 553","SIG553","FASS 90","Sig 550","Stgw 90","Sig 552","SIG 551","SIG 556","SIG SG 552","SIG SG-552","SIG SG 553","552 Commando","Commando 552","522 Commando","SSG550 Sniper","Sturmgewehr 90","SIG 552 Commando","SIG SG552 Commando","Krieg 552 Commando"],"kind":"firearm","category":"assault-rifles","subcategory":"Swiss assault rifle","description":"The SIG SG 550 is an assault rifle manufactured by SIG Sauer AG in Switzerland. \"SG\" is an abbreviation for Sturmgewehr. The SG 550 was co-developed by SIG Sauer AG and the Italian firearm manufacturer Beretta and is based on its predecessor, the SIG SG 540, which is chambered in 5.56×45mm NATO.","development":"In 1978, the Swiss armed forces formulated requirements for a successor to the Stgw 57 battle rifle (known commercially as the SG 510) using the 7.5×55mm GP 11 cartridge. Emphasis was placed on modularity; the weapon family was to include several variants of the base design, including a compact carbine that would be issued to rear-echelon and support troops, command staff, vehicle crews, special forces personnel and paratroopers. Another aim was to reduce the overall weight of the rifle while retaining comparable or improved accuracy out to 300 m. The solicitation was narrowed down to two designs: the W+F C42 (developed by the state-owned Waffenfabrik Bern, using both 6.45×48mm and 5.56×45mm cartridges) and the SG 541 (developed by SIG using the SG 540 as the basis for the SG 541 prototype). In 1981, the experimental 6.45mm GP 80 cartridge was rejected in favour of the more conventional SIG 5.6×45mm Gw Pat 90 round (with a 4.1 g, tombac-jacketed, lead core projectile) that is the Swiss equivalent to NATO's standard 5.56×45mm cartridge.","facts":[],"specifications":[{"group":"Cartridge & Action","items":[{"label":"Cartridge","value":"5.56×45mm NATOKnown as 5.6 mm Gw Pat 90 in Switzerland."},{"label":"Cartridge","value":"7.62×39mm (SG 553R, SG 556R & SIG563R)"},{"label":"Cartridge","value":".300 AAC Blackout (SG 553 BK and SIG563 BK)"},{"label":"Cartridge","value":"6.5mm Creedmoor (SG 750)","numeric":6.5,"unit":"mm"},{"label":"Action","value":"Gas operated, rotating bolt"},{"label":"Rate of Fire","value":"c. 700 rounds/min"},{"label":"Muzzle Velocity","value":"911 m/s","numeric":911,"unit":"m/s"},{"label":"Feed System","value":"Proprietary 10-, 20-, 30-round box magazines (SG 550 series & SG 560 series)"},{"label":"Feed System","value":"STANAG magazines (SIG 556 series, SG 560 series, SG 550 series (with STANAG mag well))"},{"label":"Feed System","value":"AKM-pattern magazines (SG 553R, SG 556R)"},{"label":"Feed System","value":"SG 750 series magazines (SG 750 and SG 751)"},{"label":"Sights (Rear)","value":"rotating diopter drum with tritium night inserts; front: hooded post with folding night post"},{"label":"Sights","value":"540 mm sight radius (SG 550)","numeric":540,"unit":"mm"}]},{"group":"Dimensions","items":[{"label":"Weight","value":"4.1 kg","numeric":4.1,"unit":"kg"},{"label":"Length","value":"1000 mm (stock extended)","numeric":1000,"unit":"mm"},{"label":"Length","value":"772 mm (stock folded)","numeric":772,"unit":"mm"},{"label":"Barrel Length","value":"528 mm","numeric":528,"unit":"mm"}]},{"group":"Performance","items":[{"label":"Effective Range","value":"100–400 m sight adjustments"}]},{"group":"Production","items":[{"label":"Designer","value":"Schweizerische Industrie Gesellschaft"},{"label":"Designed","value":"1970s–1980s"},{"label":"Manufacturer","value":"SIG Sauer AG (formerly Schweizerische Industrie Gesellschaft and Swiss Arms)"},{"label":"Produced","value":"1986–present"},{"label":"Number Built","value":"600,000"}]},{"group":"Service","items":[{"label":"Origin","value":"Switzerland"},{"label":"In Service","value":"1990–present"}]}],"specIndex":{"Cartridge":"5.56×45mm NATOKnown as 5.6 mm Gw Pat 90 in Switzerland.","Action":"Gas operated, rotating bolt","Rate of Fire":"c. 700 rounds/min","Muzzle Velocity":"911 m/s","Feed System":"Proprietary 10-, 20-, 30-round box magazines (SG 550 series & SG 560 series)","Sights":"rotating diopter drum with tritium night inserts; front: hooded post with folding night post","Weight":"4.1 kg","Length":"1000 mm (stock extended)","Barrel Length":"528 mm","Effective Range":"100–400 m sight adjustments","Designer":"Schweizerische Industrie Gesellschaft","Designed":"1970s–1980s","Manufacturer":"SIG Sauer AG (formerly Schweizerische Industrie Gesellschaft and Swiss Arms)","Produced":"1986–present","Number Built":"600,000","Origin":"Switzerland","In Service":"1990–present"},"images":{"hero":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/93/SIG_SG_550_Users.png","descriptionUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SIG_SG_550_Users.png","width":6460,"height":3403,"caption":"Map of nations that use the SIG SG 550","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","attribution":"TruncateVirus99"}},"gallery":[{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/93/SIG_SG_550_Users.png","descriptionUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SIG_SG_550_Users.png","width":6460,"height":3403,"caption":"Map of nations that use the SIG SG 550","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","attribution":"TruncateVirus99"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c1/C42_6.45mm_Rifle-IMG_0070-b-white.jpg","descriptionUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:C42_6.45mm_Rifle-IMG_0070-b-white.jpg","width":5616,"height":3744,"caption":"C42 6.45mm Rifle of Waffenfabrik Bern","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0 fr","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/fr/deed.en","attribution":"Rama"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e9/SIG_550_IMG_3276.jpg","descriptionUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SIG_550_IMG_3276.jpg","width":5520,"height":36